  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Congoville shows early but visible reach across public discourse, teaching, and open access use. The record includes substantial Wikipedia attention, a recorded book launch, and 1,613 OAPEN downloads, suggesting that the book is being encountered well beyond a narrow specialist audience.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest signal for Congoville is its public visibility: the work is referenced repeatedly in Wikipedia and also appears in Crossref-linked attention data, indicating that it has entered a broader knowledge ecosystem rather than remaining confined to academic catalogues alone. This kind of presence is especially relevant for a book engaged with colonial memory, urban space, and postcolonial interpretation, where public interpretation and reference often matter alongside scholarly citation.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage data reinforce that the book is being actively accessed. OAPEN reports 1,613 downloads, which points to sustained reading interest through an open-access channel. The monthly pattern shows repeated use over time rather than a single isolated spike, consistent with an audience returning to the work across the year.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Teaching-related signals are modest but meaningful. The book is discussed in a recorded book-launch video on the Middelheim Museum channel, and library holdings place it in subject areas including Modern Arts, Exhibitions, and Postcolonialism and the arts. Together, these signals suggest that the book is being taken up as a pedagogical and curatorial resource, even if the record does not show formal course adoption data.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The subject profile also aligns the book with sustainability and policy-adjacent concerns, especially Sustainable cities and communities. That fit matters because Congoville examines the urban traces of colonial history in contemporary Belgium, linking art history, exhibition practice, and public memory to questions of place, heritage, and civic imagination. With zero OpenAlex citations so far, the scholarly citation record is still early, but the wider pattern already shows public visibility, interpretive reuse, and practical relevance.</p>",
